gnees":[],"milestone":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/sqlalchemy/sqlalchemy/milestones/88","html_url":"https://github.com/sqlalchemy/sqlalchemy/milestone/88","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/sqlalchemy/sqlalchemy/milestones/88/labels","id":3942015,"node_id":"MDk6TWlsZXN0b25lMzk0MjAxNQ==","number":88,"title":"2.0beta1","description":"","creator":{"login":"zzzeek","id":128223,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjEyODIyMw==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/128223?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek","html_url":"https://github.com/zzzeek","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"open_issues":0,"closed_issues":130,"state":"closed","created_at":"2019-01-04T14:59:25Z","updated_at":"2023-07-18T12:40:05Z","due_on":null,"closed_at":"2022-10-13T22:12:01Z"},"comments":2,"created_at":"2022-02-21T02:03:38Z","updated_at":"2022-04-14T12:39:11Z","closed_at":"2022-04-14T12:39:11Z","author_association":"MEMBER","type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"### Discussed in https://github.com/sqlalchemy/sqlalchemy/discussions/7743\r\n\r\nif we have:\r\n\r\na + b\r\n\r\nthat give us BinaryExpression(a, b, operator.add).  If we do a + b+ c, that gives us BinaryExpression(BinaryExpression(a, b, +), c, +) and hence we start nesting as in #7743.\r\n\r\nYears ago we had this come up and we gave someone some kind of function to flatten this out.\r\n\r\nhowever, this can flatten automatically into a ClauseList by detecting BinaryExpression().same_operator(...) -> OperatorExpression.  this is ClauseList + ColumnElement,  we have this now already with BooleanClauseList so it would look mostly like that, except it does math operators also, we likely should use BCL and change its name.\r\n\r\nThe construction for BinaryExpression would need to check for same operator incoming and produce a single construct.  \r\n\r\nthis is not too hard and we can make it happen just for binarys that dont have anything else going on:\r\n\r\n```diff\r\ndiff --git a/lib/sqlalchemy/sql/elements.py b/lib/sqlalchemy/sql/elements.py\r\nindex 0aab04139f..4e947d4504 100644\r\n--- a/lib/sqlalchemy/sql/elements.py\r\n+++ b/lib/sqlalchemy/sql/elements.py\r\n@@ -3862,6 +3862,17 @@ class BinaryExpression(ColumnElement):\r\n \r\n     \"\"\"\r\n \r\n+    def __new__(cls, left, right, operator, **kw):\r\n+        if isinstance(left, BinaryExpression) and left.operator is operator:\r\n+            # TODO: propgate attrs, negate, modifiers?\r\n+            return BooleanClauseList._construct_raw(\r\n+                operator, [left.left, left.right, right]\r\n+            )\r\n+        else:\r\n+            obj = super().__new__(cls)\r\n+            obj.__init__(left, right, operator, **kw)\r\n+            return obj\r\n+\r\n     def __init__(\r\n         self, left, right, operator, type_=None, negate=None, modifiers=None\r\n     ):\r\n\r\n```\r\n\r\nI'd rather not use `__new__`, it can be some function like `construct_for_op` something like that, perhaps it can drive from the left side of the expr so that we dont need to use isinstance.   \r\n\r\n**tentative** 2.0 if this can go in later, it's not that involved.","closed_by":{"login":"sqlalchemy-bot","